{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: the citation design is built and green, and it does NOT give you the answer you asked for on your first case. Saying so before I artifact it rather than letting you find it in review.\n\nWHAT IT DOES, run rather than reasoned. Your reacquisition case A to B, B to A, A to C resolves at c. Permutation is fixed: forward and reversed now both give owner c, resolved, where the old head gave c and b. A genuine double spend, two sagas both citing settle-ab, is disputed at b. The quadratic look-ahead is deleted, not optimised.\n\nWHERE WE DISAGREE. Your case one was A to B, then B to C, then a late A to D, wanting resolved at c with the late A ineffective. I get disputed at a. That is deliberate and it is what I predicted in c52fbd8c: with no order to appeal to, an A to D that names no custody basis is claiming the initial owner's custody, which is exactly what A to B claims. Two spends of one grant, nothing to separate them, so the honest projection is disputed. Calling A to D \"late\" requires a clock the records do not carry, and reaching for one is how the old head ended up trusting slice index.\n\nTHE DISTINCTION YOUR FRAMING COLLAPSED, and this is the part I think is worth your attention. There are two different A-to-D's and they now get two different answers. A to D naming NOTHING competes with A to B for the genesis grant: disputed. A to D naming settle-ab, the grant B has already spent on B to C, is refused as \"offeror did not hold custody\" and the chain resolves at c with B to C settled -- I ran exactly that fixture to check. So the outcome you wanted does exist; it is the outcome for a saga that says where its custody came from. The one you described, which says nothing, is genuinely ambiguous and I will not resolve it by guessing.\n\nA CORRECTION TO MY OWN PREDICTION. In f672b14b I said case one's A to D \"cites either nothing or the same genesis A to B already spent, so it is ineffective for want of a live custody basis. Resolves at C.\" The second half of that was wrong. Citing nothing gives disputed, not ineffective; only the spent-basis variant gives ineffective. I named the wrong fixture and I am correcting it here rather than quietly shipping the behaviour I actually built.\n\nA DEFECT I INTRODUCED AND CAUGHT IN THE SAME RUN, since a green suite would otherwise imply I wrote it right first time. My first cut counted every saga naming a basis as a competitor for it, so a stranger claiming genesis custody made the owner's legitimate transfer disputed -- the same wrong-set defect you rejected the last head for, reintroduced in a new shape. My own TestNonOwnerSagaDoesNotDisputeTheOwnersTransfer failed and caught it. Candidates are now filtered by \"does this offeror actually hold at this basis\" before competition is counted.\n\nFIXTURES CHANGED, as I warned. The saga helper takes an optional custody basis; the sequential test cites settle-first. Both PRE-EXISTING tests, the cross-domain saga and the disputed-settlements one, pass unchanged, because their offers name nothing and that still means the genesis grant. Notably TestThreeStepSagaAcrossSecurityDomains already asserted that folds must not diverge by observation order; it only passed before because it holds a single completed saga.\n\nIf you want case one to resolve at c for a basis-less A to D, that is a request for an ordering source, and you would need to say what carries it. Otherwise I will artifact this. Tell me which."}